2 TITL "Kring Tycho Brahe och Hven"
1 NOTE Tycho Brahe blev f°dt i 1546 og d°de i 1601.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Man mente pσ Tycho Brahes tid at himmelverdenen var en uforanderlig st°rrelse, men den 11. november 1572, kom det til et brud med denne antagelse ΓÇô Tycho Brahes observerede fremkomsten af en ny stjerne (en supernova) i stjernebi
2 CONC lledet Cassiopeia. Hans vµrk om begivenheden, De Stella Nova, gjorde ham ber°mt i hele Europa og dermed var astronomien grundlagt som en moderne empirisk videnskab.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Tycho Brahe fik i 1576 overdraget °en Hven af Frederik 2. Her byggede han i 1580 slottet Uranienborg og fire σr senere observatoriet Stjerneborg.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Efter Frederik 2.s d°d der opstod der sσ store uoverensstemmelser mellem Christian 4.s regering og Tycho Brahe, at han forlod Danmark. Han rejste til Prag og levede her som kejserlig astronom, til sin d°d i 1601.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Tycho Brahe (eller Tyge Brahe som han blev d°bt) blev f°dt i 1546 pσ familiegodset Knudstup i Skσne som s°n af Otto Brahe og Beate Bille. Tycho Brahe voksede dog op hos faderens bror, J°rgen Brahe og hans kone, Inger Oxe (s°ster t
2 CONC il Peder Oxe) pσ godset Tostrup.
2 CONT Fra han var 7 σr fik han undervisning i latin og 12 σr gammel (i 1559) begyndte han pσ K°benhavns Universitet. I 1560 overvµrede Tycho Brahe sandsynligvis en delvis solform°rkelse, og herefter begyndte hans astronomiske studier. I
2 CONC  1562 rejste han til Leipzig for at studere jura og statsvidenskab, men istedet koncentrerede han sig om astronomi. Han opdage hurtigt store afvigelser mellem sine egne observationer af planeternes positioner og de postioner, man 
2 CONC hidtil havde antaget. Tycho Brahe vendte hjem fra Leipzig i 1565 og mellem 1566 og 1570 studerede han astronomi i Rostrock, Wittenberg, Basel og Augsburg. I denne periode konstruerede han sine f°rste astronomiske instrumenter (i 1
2 CONC 564 en radius og i 1569-70 den store kvadrant i Augsburg) og foretog mange observationer. I 1570 d°de faderen, og Tycho Brahe vendte hjem til Danmark.
2 CONT Den 11. november 1572 fik Tycho Brahe °je pσ en lysende klar stjerne i stjernebilledet Cassiopaia, som han aldrig tidligere havde set. Stjernen kunne iagttages pσ himlen i 18 mσneder. Sine observationer publicerede han i bogen De 
2 CONC Nova Stella. I dag ved vi, at det drejede sig om en supernova. I 1574 giftede Tycho sig med Kirsten J°rgensdatter. I l°bet af µgteskabet fik parret 8 b°rn. I 1575 tog han igen sydpσ, og isµr m°det med landgreve Wilhelm af Hessen, 
2 CONC som var meget interesseret i astronomi, kom til at fσ stor betydning for ham. Da han kom hjem overvejede han at forlade Danmark, men pσ opfordring af landgreven af Hessen tilb°d kongen, Frederik II, Tycho Brahe °en Hven som len og
2 CONC  dertil rigelig °konomisk st°tte (Tycho Brahes indtµgter kom til at udg°re over 1% af statens indtµgter!). I august 1576 pσbegyndtes den kombinerede bolig og observatorium Uranienborg pσ Hven. Pga. pladsmangel begyndte byggeriet a
2 CONC f observatoriet Stjerneborg i 1584 pσ en h°j udenfor Uranienborg.
2 CONT Som lensmand havde Tycho Brahe forpligtelser overfor kongen og b°nderne, men han passede ikke sine forpligtelser sµrligt godt (fx passede han ikke fyret, og han forlangte meget arbejde af sine b°nder og behandlede dem generelt dσr
2 CONC ligt).Efter Christian IV i 1596 havde overtaget regeringsmagten, mistede Tycho Brahe de fleste af sine °konomiske privilegier, men beholdt stadig Hven som len. Kritikken gik isµr pσ hans behandling af b°nderne. I 1597 forlod han D
2 CONC anmark, og i 1598 accepterde han en opfordring fra kejser Rudolf II om at bosµtte sig i Prag, hvor han kom til at arbejdede sammen med Johannes Kepler. Tycho Brahe d°de i 1601 af en urinvejsforgiftning, muligvis pga. af en kviks°l
2 CONC vforgiftning. Han er begravet i Prag. Kepler arvede Tycho Brahes observationer, hvilket satte ham i stand til at finde de keplerske love for jorden og planeternes bevµgelse omkring solen.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Three years after the death of Copernicus, one of the greatest observational astronomers was born in Copenhagen. His name was Tycho Brahe. His interest in Astronomy was decided at the age of fourteen when he observed a partial ecl
2 CONC ipse of the sun which had been predicted by contemporary astronomers. In 1572, as a student at the University of Copenhagen, he observed the sudden appearance of a very bright star, probably a supernova. Brahe was able to prove, u
2 CONC sing his own observations, that the supernova had to lie a long way outside the solar system. Such a phenomenon was unthinkable in the ancients' understanding of the heavens, which were supposed to be fixed for ever in a system of
2 CONC  perfect harmony, and Brahe's fame began to spread. In 1575 the King of Denmark gave him the island of Hven, which is within sight of Hamlet's Elsinore, with sufficient funds to establish the most advance astronomical observatory 
2 CONC of its day. Without a telescope (which was to be invented only after his death) he accumulated the most comprehensive and wonderfully accurate measurements that the world had ever seen.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Brahe seems to have been quarrelsome, opinionated and conceited. During his student days, he lost his nose in a duel and wore a gold and silver replacement for the rest of his life. During his very productive time in Hven, he was 
2 CONC a tyrannical dictator to his tenants, and the subject of a petition to the King who had recently succeeded to the throne. This king was less inclined to put up with Brahe's offensive ways than had been his father, and thelatter le
2 CONC ft Hven for good in . He moved to Prague, under the protection of Rudolph II, the king of Bohemia. Soon after the young Johannes Kepler joined him and the course of science was changed forever. Initially he was not keen on sharing
2 CONC  his observations with this young competitor, and it was only after Brahe's death, and a threatened lawsuit that Kepler, was able to obtain access to the full range of Brahe's wonderful measurements. Brahe finally succumbed to his
2 CONC  over-indulgence in food and drink by contracting a urinary infection, from which he expired.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Interestingly, Brahe did not believe in the Copernican system. The phenomenon of "parallax" occurs when you line up your finger with an object across the room; then when you move your head to right or left, your finger appears to 
2 CONC move relative to the object. In a similar way, if the earth orbited the sun, Brahe argued that the apparent position of nearer stars should change relative to that of the distant ones. His observations showed no such change. Accor
2 CONC dingly he believed in a system in which the planets moved around the sun; however, both sun and planets were considered to orbit the stationary earth. This system had the advantage that it avoided the problems associated with a sp
2 CONC inning earth. This system is mathematically very close to the Copernican but avoids the problems associated with aspinning earth.
1 NOTE Tyge Brahe was born at Knudstrup Castle, Scania, Denmark o
2 CONT
2 CONT --Other Fields
2 CONT
2 CONT EVEN: Occupation Astronomer
