1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oluf_Rosenkrantz_(baron)
2 CONT
2 CONT Oluf Baron Rosenkrantz (12 March 1623 – 13 March 1685) was a Danish landowner, councillor and critic of absolute monarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and education
2 CONT Oluf Rosenkrantz was the son of Holger Rosenkrantz "the rich" of Glimminge and Lene Mogensdatter Gyldenstierne. In 1634-40 he was a pupil at Herlufsholm. This was followed by a trip abroad. In 1641 he studied at Oxford and must be
2 CONC  assumed to have devoted himself to the reading of classical literature, modern historians and political writers. This was followed by stays in Paris, Rome, Padua and Basel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early career and landowner
2 CONT After his return he was employed as secretary in the chancellery in 1644 and held this position until 1651. In 1649 he was part of Corfitz Ulfeldt's entourage on his mission to the Netherlands. In 1657-58 he was quarterly commissi
2 CONC oner on Zealand. In 1651 he was endowed with the so-called Apostolic Estate in Norway, but did not obtain any larger fiefdom. At his father's death in 1647 he had inherited Egholm in Voldborg Herred on Zealand and in 1653 he inher
2 CONC ited the farm Demmestrup in Skåne from his brother Magnus. After he married Birgitte Krabbe, daughter of Norway's governor Gregers Krabbe and Dorthe Daa in August 1652, there was also the prospect of inheritance from this side. On
2 CONC  his father-in-law's death in 1655, Enggaard thus received the present Gyldensteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The anonymous critic
2 CONT Oluf Rosenkrantz is known to have been present at the meeting of the Estates in 1660 prior to the introduction of absolute monarchy. But despite his wealth and excellent abilities, he did not play a significant role. The conclusio
2 CONC n of peace after the Swedish wars in Copenhagen in 1660 had already affected him when he sold his Scanian farm to the Swedish nobleman Magnus Durell in 1661. This feeling of being particularly affected and also passed over was fur
2 CONC ther reinforced in the following years. Oluf Rosenkrantz had not been a personal beneficiary of the old regime, but the prospect of honour and promotion only deteriorated under the new absolute monarchy. He was not given any offic
2 CONC e. The position of commissioner at the seizure of Kaj Lykke's properties in 1661 was not a satisfactory thing for him either.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a nobleman, Oluf felt sorrow over the absolute monarchy, over the defeat of the nobility and the advance of the new elements. The blood boiled in him, and in 1663 he appeared on the political scene for the first time. The occas
2 CONC ion was a small anonymous pamphlet containing a sharp attack on the nobility with the title: "De ordine subditorum in regno Daniæ et inprimis de nobilitate epistola". Both Oluf Rosenkrantz and posterity have assumed that the autho
2 CONC r behind it was the auditor general Poul Tscherning.
2 CONT
2 CONT In response to this, the equally anonymous pamphlet "Nobilitatis responsum ad famosum factiosi calumniatoris libellum" was published. The author cannot be determined from contemporary sources, but everything indicates that traditi
2 CONC on is right in attributing it to Oluf Rosenkrantz. Not without tactical dexterity, it begins by placing the author of the letter on the nobility at the mercy of his very supposed high protectors, accusing him of wanting to pave th
2 CONC e way for the transformation of the monarchy into a democratic republic through the destruction of the nobility. Next, to have asserted the natural justification of the nobility culminates in the assertion of the injustice of accu
2 CONC sing the nobility of ignorance and idleness when they are forced to spend their lives without being used in the service of the state and are pushed back by vile, hateful parvenues.
2 CONT
2 CONT The style of the writing is marred by a bad pathos. Its content is in several respects easily attackable, but there was warmth about it, and it had to make a stir by presenting itself as a response from the entire nobility. A not 
2 CONC insignificant amount of unrest and resentment also arose in the circle of those in power. It was said that they were planning a public burning of the writing by the executioner, but the anonymous author could not be targeted, and 
2 CONC it is doubtful whether they knew who it was.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron Rosenkrantz
2 CONT But Oluf Rosenkrantz still did not get any office. In 1669 he ventured to present his opinions in his own name, albeit in a very subdued form. In a memorial to the deceased, former governor of Norway, Niels Trolle, he spoke with s
2 CONC adness of his involuntary retirement, praised the state that gave the noble honorary posts, and expressed his contempt for those who paid tribute to the abominable principle that all excellent points should be cut off. All this in
2 CONC dicates conservative pride.
2 CONT
2 CONT Så meget mærkeligere er det da, at han blot to år efter, 25. maj 1671, lod sig ophæve til baron, og dermed blev en del af enevældens nye adels- og rangorden. I kølvandet på barontitlen fulgte da en række kongelige udmærkselser. Ok
2 CONC tober samme år blev han Ridder af Dannebrog. Desuden kom en udnævnelse til etats- og justitsråd samt medlem af højesteret. Disse forfremmelser var ikke stødende eller usædvanligt for "den gamle adel", men baron-titlen stred direkt
2 CONC e imod standens traditioner og var jo også beregnet på at dele den gamle ensartede adelsstand. Om det har været forfængelighed hos Rosenkrantz, der fik ham til at bryde med sine principper kan ikke afgøres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sikkert er det, at den egentlige drivkraft var hans hustru, Birgitte Krabbe, en dame med ambitioner. Det var hende, der forhandlede med Griffenfeld om titlerne, for at ægteparret ikke i rang skulle komme til at stå tilbage for and
2 CONC re. En tid stod hun vaklende over for spørgsmålet, om der skulle forbindes et virkeligt baroni af deres gods med titlen, men da hun indså, at det var dem umuligt at oprette to sådanne baronier for deres to døtre, lod hun sig nøje 
2 CONC med titlen alene. Birgitte Krabbe skal tillige have været ganske pragtsyg. Da Holger Thott i juni 1673 holdt bryllup på Egholm med deres datter Helveg, fejredes det på den allerfineste og overdådigste måde. Samme år opkaldte Oluf 
2 CONC Rosenkrantz en på sit gods af en nyoprettet herregård efter hende med navnet Krabbesholm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selvom tilen gav Oluf Rosenkrantz nogen medvind, så havde han dog stadig skuffelser. 1673 nægtede kongen ham birkeret til Egholm, og længe måtte Birgitte Krabbe se sit allerede 1671 udtalte ønske om, at manden måtte blive gehejmer
2 CONC åd, uopfyldt. Udnævnelsen dertil kom først 1678, endda med en årlig løn på 2000 rigsdaler. Muligvis var anledningen dertil skriftet "En sandfærdig Replique" under pseudonymet Aletophilus (sandheds-elsker).[1] Skriftet var et forsv
2 CONC ar for adelsmanden Jørgen Krabbe, der var blevet henrettet af svenskerne under Den Skånske Krig. Et lignende skrift fulgte året efter til forsvar for tre andre skånske adelsmænd, Knud, Tage og Holger Thott (sidstnævnte var Rosenkr
2 CONC antz' føromtalte svigersøn), der ligeledes var blevet dømt af svenskerne.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Denne artikel bør formateres, som det anbefales i Wikipedias stilmanual. Det er med interne henvisninger, afsnitsinddeling o.l.. Husk også at tilføje kilder! (august 2013) (Lær hvordan og hvornår man kan fjerne denne skabelonbeske
2 CONC d)
2 CONT Men trods alt var der dog i O. R. langt mere end en titelsyg Hofmand. I selve Forsvarsskriftet for Jørgen Krabbe havde han ikke været bange for at angribe Kongernes fremmede Yndlinger og udtale, at alle Riger bør grundfæstes langt
2 CONC  mere paa Undersaatternes gode Vilje og Kjærlighed end paa Øvrighedens Tvang og Magt, og selv om disse Ytringer i og for sig vare uskyldige som umiddelbart anvendte paa Landets Fjender, er det dog næppe for dristigt i dem at se Ud
2 CONC tryk for Tanker, der ogsaa vare skabte ved danske Forhold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Imidlertid havde han i det hele vedblivende stærkt sysselsat sig med Læsning af politiske og historiske Forfattere, og Regeringen havde anerkjendt hans Lærdom ved 1676 at gjøre ham til Medlem af den Kommission, der skulde undersøg
2 CONC e Vitus Berings danske Krønnike. Men stærkest havde dog Bekjendtskabet rned 2 Skrifter optaget ham. Det ene var et Udtog af Verdenshistorien, forfattet af en Professor ved Gymnasiet i Lüneburg, Johan Buno, og udgivet første Gang 1
2 CONC 664, hvori Danmark var fremstillet som et oprindeligt Arverige, der kun i nyere Tid ved Adelens Overgreb var blevet til et Valgrige; det andet var en Fremstilling af den tyske Statsret, udgivet 1670 af den tyske Jurist A. P. Olden
2 CONC burger, hvori var indflettet Angreb paa den danske Adel. Dybt var O. R. bleven krænket ved disse Udgydelser; han besluttede atter at opkaste sig til sin Stands Forsvarer og lod sig berolige over for Faren herfor ved Udtalelser af 
2 CONC Overhofjægermester Hahn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apologia nobilitatis Danicæ
2 CONT Bogudgivelse
2 CONT For a long time he worked on a Latin counter-script, in Jan. 1681 he put his name on Egholm under the preface of his "Apologia nobilitatis Danicæ", this book which became the achievement and destiny of his life. He had it printed 
2 CONC in Lybek, where his little pamphlet from 1679 was also printed, but this time without a place of printing; The print run consisted of about 550 copies; in Sept. 1681 it appeared in Bogladen in Copenhagen. The writing is heavy to r
2 CONC ead, packed as it is, in the manner of the time, with tiresome erudition and long quotations from classical and modern authors, even a little old-fashioned smudged in its form. Apparently, it is fully loyal; There is plenty of pra
2 CONC ise both for former Danish kings and for Christian V. Nevertheless, there can be no doubt about its tendency. When Christian III is praised as the national king and particularly well-intentioned king against the aristocracy, and w
2 CONC hen he is praised for having used native nobles as advisers and officials, it was the difference and not the similarity between the past and the present that was on the author's mind, as well as the remarks about the foreign queen
2 CONC s who have often introduced harmful customs. did not have a purely historical origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Straight out was the defence of the nobility against the attacks that have appeared, the defence of its bravery in the wars, of its unwillingness to follow Ulfeldt's call for apostasy in 1657, of which he gave a summary, even more
2 CONC  so the attack on the wretched courtiers, who had used this cause to do everything possible to lead the nobility into misfortune and were constantly inventing new inventions to eradicate it, to which were added accusations against
2 CONC  the Germans of ingratitude, as they had always been well received in Denmark. And certainly the origin of the hereditary government was asserted, not as a return to the old conditions, but as a voluntary transfer from the estates
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In short, the meaning and spirit of the Scriptures were unquestionably, perhaps less appropriate to the rule of an absolute king. But it was written with so much attention to the expressions that it can explain why O. R. had not t
2 CONC hought of the consequences for him that it would have; for surely he was not aware of this. He was an upright, warm-hearted man, but not of those who seek martyrdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath
2 CONT The book immediately caused an extraordinary stir, its tendency was condemned by well-wishers and opponents, and provoked unrest in the old noble circles, exasperation at court. It is impossible to determine to name the author of 
2 CONC the steps that were taken, but no doubt the king himself felt offended. The government quickly intervened. In Oct., O. R. was provisionally banished from the court. In vain he tried in a petition to present the book as payment for
2 CONC  a debt of gratitude to the king and to assert its purely literary character.
2 CONT
2 CONT The procurator general Peder Scavenius presented him with 29 questions for answer; they dealt with the reason why the place of printing was not mentioned and why it had not been submitted to censorship, as well as the minutes of t
2 CONC he rebellious letter from Ulfeldt, his name with his family name despite the verdict of 1663, and to these minor things came complaints that showed that the government took the matter by the horns by wanting to understand the tend
2 CONC ency and see in it an attack both on the present regime as a which did not correspond to the pattern presented, and to its foreign officials.
2 CONT
2 CONT In vain R. resorted to denying the true meaning of his book by emphasizing that the whole thing was only historical proof of the allegiance of the nobility and of the originality of the electorate, he in vain appealed to influenti
2 CONC al statesmen to reconcile the king. In Nov. a commission of 6 councils was appointed to investigate the matter and pass judgment on it. The Fiscal General's claim to this commission was that R. should be demoted from his titles an
2 CONC d commissions, have his honour and estate forfeited, and be banished from the king's kingdoms and countries, while the book should be confiscated.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the majority of the commissioners were members of the old nobility, and no law authorized the forfeiture of the estate except in matters of life. When the verdict was handed down in Febr. 1682, it was only for forfeiture of th
2 CONC e charles and for a fine of 6000 rdl.; moreover, it was only signed by 3 of the 6 Commissioners; among the other three was Jens Juel, who had openly declared that O. R. had shown himself unworthy of being a councillor by his carel
2 CONC essness, but had not committed any offence against the sovereign.
2 CONT
2 CONT The government felt anything but satisfied with this outcome of the case; without regard to O. R.'s renewed petition, which clearly contained an offer to settle the whole matter by a voluntary monetary fine, the case was summoned 
2 CONC by the king to the Supreme Court, and here he found more willing ears. The new judgment of March p. A. committed both the crime of the charger and the main lot; O. R. was also no longer allowed to reside in Copenhagen and had to r
2 CONC ecall what was illegal in the Apology. However, shortly after the verdict, the king softened the sentence to the effect that he was to pay 20,000 rdl. from his main lot to the knightly academy that was to be established in Copenha
2 CONC gen, and the rest to go over to O.R.'s son-in-law Holger Thott as a reward for his services in the Scanian war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT During all this, Birgitte Krabbe had been ill at Egholm, and her husband had hidden the matter from her. It was not until December 1682 that she was told that Egholm had been wronged; for the sorrow over the loss of the titles, R.
2 CONC  spared her at least for a while. Deeply shaken and unhappy as she was at the thought of having to leave her farm, she seems to have been the one who in the following time did everything to save it. To that extent, Egholm also suc
2 CONC ceeded, as Egholm went over to her daughter Helle Helene, married to Counsellor Mogens Skeel, and her parents remained there; on the other hand, Enggaard was lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT O. R. himself seems to have mostly given up the game; in a Latin language of thought from 1682 he described his mood as a boredom to the world and a longing for heaven; however, his last days were embittered by the general fiscal 
2 CONC demand, of 2000 rdl. in cash as compensation for him. He died on 13. March 1685.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde: Finn Holbek: www.finnholbek.dk: til Egholm og fra 1655 til Enggård (Gyldensteen), som han fik af svigerfaderen -, 1634-1640 elev på Herlufsholm, 1641 (15. maj) i Oxford, senere til Frankrig med broderen, derefter i Italien 
2 CONC m. Joh. Fr. Sellinger fra Strasbourg som præceptor, 1644 (30. april) Rom, s.å. (28. maj) Padua, s.å. Basel, 1644-1651 sekr. i kancelliet, ledsagede 1649 Corfitz Ulfeldt til Holland, 1651 forlenet m. apostelgodset i Norge, 1653-165
2 CONC 8 kvartalskommissær på Sjælland, solgte 1661 Demmestrup, som han 1653 havde fået af broderen Mogens, var meget harm over, hvad han anså for adelens ydmygelse. Udgav bl.a. et modskrift mod et angreb på adelen, fik intet embede før 
2 CONC 1671, var vedvarende modstander af absolutismen. Optoges 25. maj 1671 i friherrelig stand, s.å. hvid ridder, etats- og justitsråd, assessor i Højesteret, oprettede 1673 hovedgården Krabbesholm (Volborg h.), 1678 gehejmeråd, 1682 f
2 CONC radømt sine charger og idømt en stor bøde for sin kendte bog »Apologia Nobilitatis Danicæ« (1681).
2 CONT
2 CONT Oluf Rosenkrantz var en lärd man och en mycket kompetent författare. Han var också skåning från en av landskapets mest kända släkter, Rosenkrantzarna till Glimmingehus. I januari 1678 avrättades hans gode vän och släkting baron Jö
2 CONC rgen Krabbe till Krageholm (Krogholm) av svenskarna. Rosenkrantz skrev då under pseudonymen "Aletophilus" en bok till Krabbes försvar. Möjligen var detta på uppmaning av kung Christian V som efteråt gav honom 2000 rdl, förmodligen
2 CONC  i belöning. Samma år dömdes även Oluf Rosenkrantz svärson Holger Thott samt dennes bröder Knud och Tage till döden av svenskarna  i sin frånvaro - de hade hunnit fly till danska områden. Rosenkrantz skrev då en försvarsskrift om 
2 CONC dem också .
2 CONT Ett bevis på att Oluf Rosenkrantz var en respekterad författare och historiker är att han 1676 utnämndes till medlem av den kommission som skulle granska Vitus Berings danska kungakrönika "Florus Danicus" som sedan gavs ut 1698. 
2 CONT Källa: Bøggild-Andersen, C. O.: Oluf Rosenkrantz i Dansk Biografisk Leksikon på lex.dk. Hentet 29. juli 2026 fra https://biografiskleksikon.lex.dk/Oluf_Rosenkrantz
